WEBVTT
TITLE Sets and Set Operations — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
A set is a collection of distinct objects. The objects are its elements.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Order and repetition are not part of a set: {1, 2, 3} and {3, 1, 2, 2} are the same set.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Write x in A when x is an element of A, and x not in A when it is not.

4
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Roster form lists the elements. Set-builder form states the rule an element must satisfy.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
The empty set has no elements at all. It is written as the empty-set symbol, or as a pair of braces with nothing between them.

6
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:45.000
The set holding zero is not empty. It has one element, the number zero.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:53.000
The set holding the empty set is also not empty. Its one element is the empty set.

8
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:02.000
So the empty set and the set holding the empty set are different sets: sizes zero and one.

9
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:10.000
A is a subset of B when every element of A is also an element of B.

10
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Read the definition as an implication: for every x, if x is in A then x is in B.

11
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:27.000
The empty set is a subset of every set, because there is no element of it that could fail the test.

12
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Two sets are equal exactly when each is a subset of the other. That is the double-inclusion method.

13
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:44.000
The power set of A is the set whose elements are all the subsets of A.

14
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:52.000
If A has n elements, the power set has two to the n elements.

15
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Here are all eight subsets of a three-element set, produced by enumeration in this page.

16
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.000
The power set of the empty set holds one element, the empty set, and two to the zero is one.

17
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:16.000
The union holds everything that is in A or in B, including what is in both.

18
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:24.000
The intersection holds what is in both. The difference holds what is in A and not in B.

19
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:32.000
The complement of A is everything in the universe that is not in A.

20
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:42.000
One concrete pair, computed five ways. The number under each result is its size.

21
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:50.000
The complement of a union is the intersection of the complements.

22
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:58.000
The complement of an intersection is the union of the complements.

23
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:06.000
Both laws come from one logical law, applied element by element.

24
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:14.000
So set identities are logical identities in disguise, and the truth table you already know settles them.

25
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Adding the two sizes counts the overlap twice, so subtract it once.

26
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:30.000
For three sets, add the three sizes, subtract the three pairwise overlaps, add the triple overlap back.

27
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Two bounds follow at once: the union is at least as large as either set, and at most the sum of the sizes.

28
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:48.000
The same subtraction gives the difference and the symmetric difference.

29
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:56.000
The Cartesian product is the set of all ordered pairs, first coordinate from A and second from B.

30
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:04.000
Its size is the product of the sizes: every first coordinate is paired with every second coordinate once.

31
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Swapping the two sets changes the product whenever the sets differ.

32
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:20.000
A product with the empty set is empty: there is no second coordinate to pair anything with.

33
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:28.000
Fix the universe and put its three elements in a fixed order. A subset becomes a row of three bits.

34
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:37.000
Eight rows, eight subsets. That is where two to the n comes from.

35
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:46.000
The same picture counts subset pairs: each element has three choices, so there are three to the n pairs with A inside B.

36
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.000
One: membership is the primitive. Subset is an implication over members.

37
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.000
Two: the empty set is not the set holding zero, and not the set holding itself.

38
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Three: the union size subtracts the overlap, and the product size multiplies the sizes.

